-4a31064b55d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050587 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050587 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050587 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050587 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050587 #edb/ref 1031 "Computing Probabilities for Compound Events Using Venn Diagrams" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050587 #edb/ref 1033 794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050587 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5608f963-4aa6-51e0-b27f-7406f85dcd4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1031 "The Intersection of Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1033 2827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7c048599-260c-5867-88ec-692aa4d19c71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081119 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081119 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081119 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081119 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081119 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081119 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081119 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081119 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186081122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081119 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "20fa1430-8dd3-5d74-994e-23d7c1d35350" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081120 #edb/ref 1080 "images/72/72bc783c78e78798c87b8eee57ede740c8df18f0f6e83e0065497eedc2511a40.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081120 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081120 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "95c48943-e8cf-52eb-8c3a-7075385c4728" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081121 #edb/ref 1080 "images/74/74aa1c8d177607476f6bc2f87eaf3b4020d797a47f8559744e3999820bc0aa90.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081121 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081121 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ee6e5fb5-b24b-5f6b-9344-3e008fe670ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081122 #edb/ref 1080 "images/3d/3da8b95c5182be1fd4c027e5e6f2ce84274286c805afdc84891bd5aecd14add9.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081122 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081122 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fb13240a-7e95-58e7-be19-827e2f2083df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081123 #edb/ref 1080 "images/5c/5c2828cff69078cb82222b981b261010e2c0dc7f2f688945ded9a967402fa478.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081123 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081123 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fdbc6bae-4679-515d-b6ab-6d20fd308a16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081124 #edb/ref 1080 "images/69/6938c3f414dc4fa9b110d0babae434ea8079f065d121c1710c6a7d91f10dafd9.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081124 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186081124 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2cf145f5-c06d-551a-b6e2-efe50e6ef734" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082919 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082919 #edb/ref 1071 "A dietician has $25$ patients, $10$ of whom are smokers, and $12$ of whom have diabetes. None of the diabetic patients are smokers. Represent the corresponding sample space using a Venn diagram." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082919 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6616ee7f-30ed-54ef-b578-b33bdb0101bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082919 #edb/ref 1074 "Let us call $A$ the event \"The patient is a smoker\" and $B$ the event \"The patient has diabetes.\" \n\nSince none of the diabetic patients are smokers, we draw two non-overlapping circles to represent the events $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Since there are $10$ smokers, we write the number $10$ within the circle $A.$\n\n- Since there are $12$ patients with diabetes, we write the number $12$ within the circle $B.$\n\nThere are $25$ patients in total, so the number of remaining patients is \n\n$$\n25 - 10 - 12 = 3.\n$$\n\nTo represent the $3$ remaining patients, we write the number $3$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nFinally, our Venn diagram looks as follows:\n\n![](images/86/86855ecd3f1199c9a5c6fbb66ccf08d5b8dd2913d358f52d2c499039ea6fda4d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082920 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082920 #edb/ref 1071 "The Venn diagram shown below represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes on each region of the sample space. Given that every outcome is equally likely, find $P(A\\cup B).$\n\n![](images/34/34ab66b54fcf6be9740be1fc89bd436ccc8d334dc8b20a5769993a93bd05b2f9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082920 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad342550-d410-529f-a5c0-5b67845cff81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082920 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A\\cup B$ is represented by the area that is in either circle (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/b2/b25a0b74f7b5242f783c38c81bdb4bf8025a847f7079fc90febe510fec041b6d.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A \\cup B$ is\n\n$$\n20+15+25=60.\n$$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n20+15+25+40=100.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\dfrac{60}{100}= 0.6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082925 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13212" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082925 #edb/ref 1071 "A survey conducted among $100$ students of a music school found that $68$ of them play piano, $39$ of them play violin, and there are $22$ students who play both instruments. Draw a Venn diagram representing this sample space. If a student is randomly selected among the $100$ students, what is the probability that they play at least one of these instruments?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fcdd0c3b-36f8-5452-820a-114a8e70aa49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082925 #edb/ref 1074 "Let us call $A$ the event \"The student plays piano\" and $B$ the event \"The student plays violin.\"\n\nNote that there are $22$ students who play both piano and violin. We draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $22$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who play *only* the piano is\n\n$$\n68 - 22 = 46,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $46$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of students who play *only* the violin is\n\n$$\n39 - 22 = 17,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $17$ in the part of the circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A.$\n\nThe total number of students who play at least one of the instruments is\n\n$$\n22+46+17 = 85,\n$$\n\nand there are $100$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is\n\n$$\n100 - 85 = 15.\n$$\n\nTo represent these $15$ students who play neither piano nor violin, we write the number $15$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nTherefore, our Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](images/cc/cc61c2a5c61467ac19d5debe2c2e53dc48f06f8d153c014f4a73e6feed514900.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to students who play at least one of the instruments. The probability that a randomly selected student is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP\\left(A\\cup B\\right)=\\dfrac{85}{100}= 0.85.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083390 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083390 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083390 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083390 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083390 #edb/ref 1039 "Creating a Venn Diagram Given a Description of a Sample Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083390 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083390 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c6820924-ae0c-5bd0-b374-ef7b5badcc47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083391 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083391 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083391 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083391 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083391 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083391 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083391 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083391 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Probability Using a Venn Diagram" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083391 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083391 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fbd8cd17-5aed-557e-b301-9828d84745c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083392 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Probability Given a Description of a Sample Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083392 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083392 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c9401702-a2d9-5c53-a3de-8109a75111d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083471 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083471 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083471 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083471 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083471 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083471 #edb/ref 1045 "Computing Probabilities for Compound Events Using Venn Diagrams" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083471 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083471 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083471 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083471 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3807724b-f201-5101-ab81-a4cf26b5a6fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083698 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186081119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083698 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42349" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083698 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083698 #edb/ref 1071 "A language class has $63$ students, $43$ of them are studying English, and $25$ of them are studying Spanish. There are $10$ students studying both languages.\n\nIf $A$ represents the event that a student is studying English, and $B$ represents the event that a student is studying Spanish, which of the following gives the correct Venn diagram for the corresponding sample space?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083698 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "082ab98d-c2c3-56cd-8f7b-54c7956b64aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083698 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that there are $10$ students who study both languages. So, we draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $10$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who *only* study English is\n\n$$\n43-10=33,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $33$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of students who *only* study Spanish is\n\n$$\n25-10=15,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $15$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A.$\n\nThere are $63$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is \n\n$$\n63-10-33-15=5.\n$$\n\nTo represent the $5$ remaining students, we write the number $5$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nFinally, our Venn diagram looks as follows:\n\n![](images/3d/3da8b95c5182be1fd4c027e5e6f2ce84274286c805afdc84891bd5aecd14add9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084374 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084374 #edb/ref 1061 59918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084374 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0419327a-0fde-5cc1-b922-39de54c895bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084374 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084375 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084375 #edb/ref 1061 59919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084375 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2675beda-8eb1-5e62-a656-8901b8c74f2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084375 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084376 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084376 #edb/ref 1061 59922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084376 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52b66831-dec9-58df-b2e0-185b933ab681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084376 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084377 #edb/ref 1061 59925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084377 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41926e22-9aaf-53ef-ae46-08eca29ed619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084377 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084508 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a bag of tiles labeled $a,b,c,d,e,$ and $f,$ where the bag contains an equal number of each tile. We draw a single tile at random from the bag. The sample space $\\mathcal S$ of all possible outcomes is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal S = \\{ a, b, c, d, e, f \\}.\n$$\n\nLet's define two events, $A$ and $B,$ as follows:\n\n- Let $A$ be the event that we draw a vowel. Then, we have\n\n$$\nA = \\{ a, e \\}.\n$$\n\n- Let $B$ be the event that we get one of the first three letters of the alphabet. Then, we have\n\n$$\nB = \\{ a, b, c \\}.\n$$\n\nHow do we calculate $P(A\\cap B)?$\n\nLet's use a Venn diagram representation for $A\\cap B,$ as shown below. Notice that we haven't included the probabilities, just the values contained within $\\mathcal S.$\n\n![](images/02/027955ed4e60b71c008c054c29c47bdaf092c4f4aff255327ea3c5bda7788f60.png)\n\nLet's construct another Venn diagram in which we count the number of entries in each region of the Venn diagram above.\n\n![](images/43/43790d39be47480338fdd4df095854de3757b36ce9bc659d60f9b3a6e8688269.png)\n\nWe're told that there is an equal number of each tile, so every outcome in the sample space is equally likely. Therefore, to compute $P(A \\cap B),$ we need to count the number of events in $A \\cap B$ and divide by the total number of events in $\\mathcal S.$\n\n- In a Venn diagram, $A \\cap B$ is represented by the area where the circles overlap. From the diagram above, we see that there is only $\\color{red}1$ event in $A \\cap B.$\n\n- The total number of events in $\\mathcal S$ is $1+1+2+2={\\color{blue}{6}}.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = \\frac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}6}.\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region representing the event we're interested in and divide by the total number of outcomes in the sample space $\\mathcal S.$ \n\nFinally, it is important to remember that this method works *only* if every outcome in the sample space is equally likely." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084508 #edb/ref 1065 8171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084508 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8155ae13-9be9-576a-a096-d3c633e6e865" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084508 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559035 #edb/ref 1080 "0.15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559035 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559035 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09cc727b-a898-5540-97d6-f2c9b147974f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559039 #edb/ref 1080 "0.35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559039 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559039 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b0f1776-a062-5fb8-a2c8-2000dc8577c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559045 #edb/ref 1080 "0.43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559045 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559045 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d507738-9fa2-5896-a470-f0b105f24c04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559072 #edb/ref 1080 "0.33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559072 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559072 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18daee96-9926-51bc-aa9c-54bcce231698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559104 #edb/ref 1080 "0.72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559104 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559104 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "23feca0a-70cb-543d-b0d9-51f705b682a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559120 #edb/ref 1080 "0.85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559120 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559120 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "297c5b88-2fb7-580a-8e17-ad860e113bd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559122 #edb/ref 1080 "images/a5/a511835f1ecf6a7f3991f8ebb9ed14339ebfab4cb41aa06363502df5abf03719.png" #edb/ref 1759218604