92186045417] [17592186559658 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559658 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559658 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2259f512-8e22-5ed6-bb49-42aefe325560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559671 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559671 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559671 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559671 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559671 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559671 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559671 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559671 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559671 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "332525cc-a511-5267-bb34-aaa17e24cd11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559733 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559733 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559733 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559733 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a0501cb1-59d9-5e7f-87d0-8d8554465408" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559738 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559738 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559738 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559738 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559738 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559738 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559738 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559738 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559738 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ae364575-09e6-5cf9-9c82-8eb16e6ba56e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559777 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559777 #edb/ref 1069 "q-11627" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559777 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559777 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/20/20aaaae06d88f9105276f77bb65975e7d775c8fd15fbf4984d9b1889e73503dd.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space. Given that every outcome is equally likely, find $P(A\\cap B)$ to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559777 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b138f98-60f4-5926-8616-f98d6e7b3754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559777 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A\\cap B$ is represented by the area that is inside the overlap between the circles (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/c0/c0c7df273c9b71fc0286b03d7405cb8ca473d14b1020fc1ec15530879ddb8f1c.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A\\cap B$ is $15.$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n20+15+25+40=100.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B)=\\frac{15}{100}=\\frac{3}{20}=0.15\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559837 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559837 #edb/ref 1069 "q-11631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559837 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559837 #edb/ref 1071 "At a band audition, there are $50$ singers. There are $27$ country singers, $23$ pop singers, and $6$ country and pop singers. Draw a Venn diagram representing this sample space. If a singer is selected at random among the $50$ singers, what is the probability that the singer sings country or pop music (or both)? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559837 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ac83742-fbb3-5922-b2ad-70bce502b290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559837 #edb/ref 1074 "Let us call $A$ the event \"the singer sings country music\" and $B$ the event \"the singer sings pop music.\"\n\nNote that there are $6$ singers singing both country and pop music. So, we draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $6$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of singers who *only* sing country music is\n\n$$\n27-6=21,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $21$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of singers who *only* sing pop music is\n\n$$\n23-6=17,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $17$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A.$\n\nThe total number of singers who sing country or pop music is\n\n$$\n6+21+17=44,\n$$\n\nand there are $50$ singers in total, so the number of remaining singers is\n\n$$\n50-44=6.\n$$\n\nTo represent these $6$ singers who did not sing country or pop music, we write the number $6$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nTherefore, our Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](images/02/0240b83c3de5c8c3f3b1e91d3b9c8ae2899f0b2a18611d7e70b6dbf71f23e25b.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to singers that sing country or pop music (or both). The probability that a randomly selected singer is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\frac{44}{50}=0.88.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559850 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559850 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559850 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559850 #edb/ref 1071 "In a school, a survey conducted on the $40$ students shows that $17$ students traveled to school by train, $23$ students traveled by bus, and $10$ students traveled by both train and bus. \n\nIf $T$ represents the event that a student travels by train, and $B$ represents the event that a student travels by bus, which of the following diagrams gives the correct Venn diagram for the corresponding sample space?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559850 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b91a0507-397b-5914-a663-1432f07abc1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559850 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that there are $10$ students who traveled by both train and bus. We draw two overlapping circles representing the events $T$ and $B$ and write the number $10$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who traveled by train *only*  is\n\n$$\n17-10=7,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $7$ in the part of circle $T$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of students who traveled by bus *only*  is\n\n$$\n23-10=13,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $13$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $T.$\n\nThere are $40$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is \n\n$$\n40-7-10-13=10.\n$$\n\nTo represent the $10$ remaining students, we write the number $10$ outside of the circles $T$ and $B.$\n\nFinally, our Venn diagram looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a5/a511835f1ecf6a7f3991f8ebb9ed14339ebfab4cb41aa06363502df5abf03719.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559854 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c5/c5f197f52df26da43b27b2b142f25462570828996ea28154f865bac2e871e972.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space. Given that every outcome is equally likely, find $P(A\\cup B)$ to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c12c5e9b-72e8-5854-9bf8-a8cf7ba5e8c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559854 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A\\cup B$ is represented by the area that is inside both circles (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/ad/ad51768e560488687b0dfbd573f71136f7863106d0a830828878bc5488b05daf.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A\\cup B$ is\n\n$$\n6+1+4=11.\n$$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n6+1+4+12=23.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\frac{11}{23}≈0.48\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559860 #edb/ref 1071 "In a class of $30$ students, $18$ have black hair, $9$ have blue eyes and $7$ have both black hair and blue eyes. Draw a Venn diagram representing this sample space. If a student is selected at random among the $30$ students, what is the probability that the student has black hair or blue eyes? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc44bf24-a689-5cdc-ad9e-c41be1cff05a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559860 #edb/ref 1074 "Let us call $A$ the event \"the student has black hair\" and $B$ the event \"the student has blue eyes.\"\n\nNote that there are $7$ students who have both black hair and blue eyes. So, we draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $7$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who *only* have black hair is\n\n$$\n18-7=11,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $11$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of students who *only* have blue eyes is\n\n$$\n9-7=2,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $2$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A.$\n\nThere are $30$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is \n\n$$\n30-11-7-2=10.\n$$\n\nTo represent these $10$ students that have not black hair or blue eyes, we write the number $10$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nTherefore, our Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](images/fa/fab4a1a0244dc20c692101b35bac04ca9e73f4458a709d2507ba8d9a21345109.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to students that have black hair or blue eyes. The probability that a randomly selected student is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\frac{11+7+2}{30}=\\frac{20}{30}≈0.67,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
